    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Proponents of \"race realism\" argue that IQ differences between racial groups are due to genetic variations that have evolved over time, with certain groups possessing more desirable traits. They often cite the work of Hans Eysenck, who claimed that IQ is heritable and that certain racial groups have more favorable genetic makeup. They also point to the idea of \"evolutionary adaptation,\" suggesting that different racial groups have adapted to their respective environments in unique ways, resulting in differences in intelligence. Additionally, they often reference the work of Charles Murray and Richard Herrnstein's now-discredited book \"The Bell Curve,\" which proposed that IQ scores are influenced by genetics and that certain racial groups are more prone to certain cognitive abilities.",
