      "content": "Proponents of \"race realism\" often frame their arguments as a settled scientific debate, despite the overwhelming consensus among scientists that genetic differences between racial groups do not account for IQ differences. They tend to dismiss or downplay the role of socioeconomic factors, education, and cultural influences, instead relying on cherry-picked studies and flawed methodologies to support their claims. They also frequently cite the work of a handful of researchers, such as J.P. Rushton and Richard Lynn, who have been widely discredited and debunked by the scientific community. By presenting their arguments as a mainstream scientific perspective, they attempt to legitimize their claims and undermine the credibility of opposing views.",
      "source": "target",
